export const LESSONS={"spread": {"title": "Give spread its frequency.", "objective": "CH5-01 · Calculate sample monthly SD and qualify an annualised volatility estimate.", "body": "<p><strong>Volatility</strong> here means the sample standard deviation of monthly total returns. Work in percentage units: 2 means 2%; the SD is in percentage points. Subtract the arithmetic mean, square each deviation, add, divide by n−1 and take the square root. At least two observations are needed.</p><div class=\"formula\">s monthly = √[Σ(r − mean r)² / (n−1)]<br>s annualised = s monthly × √12</div><p>Worked example [−2,0,2]%: mean 0, squared deviations 4+0+4=8, variance 8/2=4, SD 2 percentage points. The annualised estimate is 2×√12≈6.928 percentage points. Multiplying by 12 would give 24 and use the wrong scaling rule.</p><p>The square-root-of-time rule assumes independent, identically distributed monthly returns with stable variance; serial dependence or changing volatility can invalidate it. The scaling is a model convention, not a realised one-year loss bound or a guarantee. Six invented months are a particularly weak basis for a future risk estimate.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> shift every return upward by 1 percentage point. The mean rises, but the deviations and SD stay unchanged. Higher average return need not mean higher measured volatility. SD treats upside and downside deviations symmetrically and cannot describe every risk.</p>", "reflection": "Show the sample denominator and units. Explain why the annual scaling is conditional and why SD is not a maximum-loss forecast.", "criteria": "I used n−1, monthly units, √12 and a material scaling limitation."}, "drawdown": {"title": "The route matters.", "objective": "CH5-02 · Track wealth, its running peak and the deepest observed loss from a peak.", "body": "<p>A <strong>drawdown</strong> is the fall from the highest wealth reached up to that date. Include initial wealth in the peak history. We report each drawdown as a nonpositive percentage and maximum drawdown as a <strong>positive loss magnitude</strong>. Other software may use negative maximum values; declare the convention.</p><div class=\"formula\">Wealth t = wealth t−1 × (1 + return t/100)<br>Peak t = max(initial wealth, wealth 1,…, wealth t)<br>Drawdown t (%) =100 × (wealth t / peak t −1)</div><p>Worked path A from 100: [−20%,+25%] gives 100→80→100. Peaks 100→100→100, drawdowns 0→−20%→0; maximum magnitude 20%. Path B [0%,0%] also ends 100 but never falls below 100; maximum drawdown 0%. Same ending wealth does not describe the experience along the way.</p><p>Another path [+25%,−20%] goes 100→125→100: maximum drawdown is 20%, measured from 125. A return of−20% needs a subsequent+25% to recover; losses and recoveries use different bases. Never add percentages to recover wealth.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> can a future loss exceed this observed maximum? Yes. It covers only sampled dates; intramonth falls are hidden by monthly data. External deposits or withdrawals require cash-flow-aware performance accounting and are absent here.</p>", "reflection": "Trace a peak and loss from initial wealth. Contrast same ending wealth with different drawdowns and state a sampling limitation.", "criteria": "I included initial wealth, stated the positive magnitude convention, and did not call historical drawdown a future bound."}, "sharpe": {"title": "Declare the excess-return ratio.", "objective": "CH5-03 · Use matching-frequency excess returns and handle an undefined denominator.", "body": "<p>The <strong>Sharpe ratio</strong> summarises mean differential return relative to its variability. Here the differential return is each portfolio monthly total return minus a fixed matching monthly risk-free return. It is unitless because numerator and denominator use the same percentage-point units.</p><div class=\"formula\">Excess t = portfolio monthly return t − monthly risk-free<br>Monthly Sharpe = mean(excess) / sample SD(excess)<br>Annualised Sharpe = √12 × monthly Sharpe</div><p>Worked example monthly returns [−1,1,3]%, risk-free 0%: excess mean 1 and sample SD 2, so monthly Sharpe .5 and annualised Sharpe√12×.5≈1.732. A fixed risk-free shift changes the mean but not the SD. If every excess return is identical, SD=0 and Sharpe is <strong>undefined</strong>; report that explicitly.</p><p>When given an <strong>annual effective</strong> risk-free rate R%, convert with 100×[(1+R/100)^(1/12)−1]. At 12% annual effective, the monthly rate is approximately 0.948879%, not 1%. Subtracting the full annual rate from every monthly return mixes frequencies.</p><p>Annualising Sharpe by√12 assumes suitably independent stationary monthly differential returns. Serial correlation can break that scaling. A large sample ratio does not prove skill; estimation error, omitted costs, negative averages and non-normal downside risks matter. The risk-free proxy itself has a currency and horizon; this simulation uses a constant hypothetical proxy.</p>", "reflection": "State the numerator, sample denominator, effective annual-to-monthly conversion and undefined case. Explain a scaling or omitted-risk limitation.", "criteria": "I matched frequencies and units, used sample SD, and treated zero denominator as undefined."}, "benchmark": {"title": "Make a fair comparison.", "objective": "CH5-04 · Compare compound growth and benchmark-relative results on matching dates and basis.", "body": "<p>A <strong>benchmark</strong> is a declared comparator, chosen before judging the result because it represents a relevant opportunity or risk exposure. Match dates, return frequency, currency, distribution treatment and fee basis. Do not select the best-looking period for one series.</p><div class=\"formula\">Total return (%) =100 × (ending wealth / initial wealth −1)<br>Difference in percentage points = portfolio total return − benchmark total return</div><p>Worked example both start 100 on the same date: portfolio ends 108, benchmark 105. Their total returns are 8% and 5%; the difference is 3 percentage points. The ratio 108/105−1≈2.857% answers a different relative-wealth question. Label the measure used.</p><p>The arithmetic average monthly return is not compound growth. Returns+20%,−20% have arithmetic mean 0% yet wealth 100→120→96, a−4% total return. A benchmark comparison must retain this timing and compounding.</p><p>Read the supplied six-month A/B table as portfolio and benchmark. Both are hypothetical monthly total returns with distributions included; fees, taxes, trading costs and flows are absent. A risk dashboard can show compound wealth, monthly SD, annualised SD, observed maximum drawdown and the declared Sharpe side by side; no single score settles suitability.</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> is outperforming a mismatched benchmark evidence of skill? No. Exposure differences, chance, sample selection and omitted frictions remain alternative explanations. Report underperformance honestly; a favourable conclusion is not the learning objective.</p>", "reflection": "Specify what must match, distinguish a percentage-point difference from a relative wealth ratio, and write one conditional comparison.", "criteria": "I matched dates and return basis, compounded wealth, and avoided a skill or future-performance claim."}};
export const SOURCES=[["NIST: sample standard deviation and units", "https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda356.htm"], ["Sharpe: The Sharpe Ratio (1994)", "https://web.stanford.edu/~wfsharpe/art/sr/sr.htm"]];
